 - **The installer is a bash script** whose body is a function called on its last line. `curl … | bash` runs it, and bash then has the whole script before any command it runs can read the rest of standard input. It treats the directory beside it as a checkout only when that directory's `Cargo.toml` names the package `trip`.
 - **A release build takes about 10 seconds** on an Apple M2 Max, well under § 3's estimate of a minute or two. `TRIP_RELEASE=1 cargo build --release --locked` took 8.6 seconds at `0.1.0`, from a clean target directory with the crates already in cargo's cache. The README gives that figure.
+- **The release pull request needs no approval.** § 2.1 first recorded a ruleset that required one approving review. The live `main` ruleset, read with `gh api repos/ninjudd/trip/rulesets/21176674`, requires a pull request with `required_approving_review_count` 0, resolved review threads, linear history, and squash merges, and forbids deleting or force-pushing `main`. It requires no status check. So the propose job posts no status of its own, unlike Projector's, which signs off its `projector/review` status. If the ruleset comes to require an approval, you can give it yourself, because the workflow's token opened the pull request and pushed its only commit.
+- **Only a push that raises the version releases anything.** The publish job runs on every push to `main` that changes `Cargo.toml`, and runs `release.py tag --commit "$GITHUB_SHA" --since "$BEFORE"` with the push's previous head. `tag` returns without tagging unless `--commit` names a higher version than `--since`. Projector's job releases any untagged version instead, which here would publish the never-released `0.1.0` the first time a dependency bump or this stack's own `Cargo.toml` change reached `main`. A push that lowers the version, as merging GitHub's revert of a release pull request does, releases nothing either, so reverting the first release does not publish `0.1.0`, and reverting a later one leaves `v0` on the release it names.
+- **A version below the newest release is refused, and `v0` never moves back.** `tag` refuses a new version below the newest `vX.Y.Z` tag on the remote, such as `0.5.1` after a revert from `0.6.0` to `0.5.0`, and says to set a version above `0.6.0`. A version that already has its tag is a release to finish, not a new one, so a re-run still completes it. When the major tag already names a newer version, as after re-running an older release's failed job once a newer release has shipped, `tag` finishes the older release and leaves the major tag where it is. The propose job still bumps from the version on `main`, so after a revert it would open a pull request the publish job refuses. Open that release pull request by hand instead, with a version above the newest release that `release.py set` writes.
+- **`tag` releases the commit the push made**, not whatever `main` holds when the job runs, so a re-run after later merges still tags the release commit. `tag` refuses a `--commit` that is not on `origin/main`. Without `--commit`, as when you run it by hand, it releases `origin/main`'s head, as Projector's does.
+- **`check-commit` checks what the release commit changes, not only which files.** It refuses a commit unless it changes exactly `Cargo.toml` and `Cargo.lock`, raises the version, and leaves both files as they were apart from `[package]`'s version and `trip`'s entry in `Cargo.lock`. Projector's propose job compares only the list of changed files, in shell. Here the check is a `release.py` subcommand, so `tests/release_test.py` covers it.
+- **The version is bumped as text, and checked as TOML.** `release.py` reads both versions with `tomllib` and refuses files that disagree or a `Cargo.lock` without exactly one `trip` package. It rewrites only the `version` line of Cargo.toml's `[package]` table and the one that follows `name = "trip"` in `Cargo.lock`, so every other byte, including another package at the same version, stays as it was. `--locked` builds in the Check workflow fail on a `Cargo.lock` left at the old version, which the release branch's dispatched check would show.
+- **`check.yml` runs `python3 tests/release_test.py`** as a step of its own after `cargo fmt --check`, rather than naming it `*_e2e.py`: it tests a script, needs no `trip` build, and runs in about 8 seconds. It needs Python 3.11 or newer for `tomllib`, which the `macos-latest` and `ubuntu-latest` runners have. The release jobs run on `ubuntu-latest`, since they build nothing.
